(vector
 (map
  (:adr/id "adr-2609112900-the-template-was-not-unstable-it-was-stale")
  (:adr/title "The template was not unstable, it was stale")
  (:adr/status "accepted")
  (:adr/date "2026-09-11")
  (:adr/related (vector "adr-2609112700-a-refusal-scoped-wider-than-its-reason"))
  (:adr/body "
## The item

Ranked as the layer-0 bottleneck: the generator's template was not a fixed
point of its own generator. It had the most downstream, because every
future block regeneration would keep skipping it.

## The framing was wrong, and the measurement says so

I described it as a structural instability. It is not. gen(gen(x)) = gen(x)
-- measured by feeding the regenerated component back in as the template
and getting byte-identical output. The generator is idempotent.

What was actually true is duller and more dangerous: the template was
STALE. Two fixes had landed and reached all nineteen other repositories,
and the twentieth -- the one the defective text had come from -- never
received them, because nothing regenerates the template.

Measured, before and after, on the component compiled to aarch64-macos and
executed:

    oracle-filters   5,5,5,5,5,5   ->  5,5,0,1,0,5
    oracle-handlers  sel6 400      ->  sel6 200
                     sel13 7       ->  sel13 727477728

Those are exactly the two defect signatures repaired across the cohort in
the two preceding iterations. The template had both.

## Why updating it is safe

Generating com-agones from the old template and from the regenerated one
produces byte-identical output. The regions that differ between the two
templates are precisely the blocks the generator rewrites anyway, so the
template's content there never reaches a downstream repository.

That control had to be run before landing. Without it the change could
have altered nineteen components through a path nobody was looking at.

## The part that is not a fix

com-abb-robotics has a hand-written harness, and it compares filters and
handlers with its own literals over its own seeded rows.

Handler selector 6 previously DISAGREED with it -- harness 200, component
400. It now agrees. The harness was right and the component wrong, and the
disagreement had been sitting there.

Filter agreement improves from three of six to four of six. Selectors 1
and 2 still differ, and they are not comparable by construction: the
harness asks for a name that three of its rows carry, the component asks
for a value that all five of its rows carry. Different questions over
different seeds. Reporting that as parity would be false, and aligning
them means converging the hand-written harness, which is a separate
decision.

## What stops it recurring

A check, because the one-time regeneration fixes today and not the next
time a block is added -- which is exactly how this happened.

    kbb --backend sci scripts/verify-template-fixed-point.cljk <com-abb-robotics checkout>

    stale template   exit 1  STALE, first differing line and both lengths
    regenerated      exit 0  FIXED-POINT
    no argument      exit 2  REFUSED

All three exercised. It reports the FIRST differing line rather than a
count of positionally-unequal lines: that count reads as a magnitude and
is an artefact of the comparison, measuring 89 here for a diff that was 23
added and 7 removed. It regenerates into a copy, because a check that
mutates its subject cannot be run twice.

## What this changes about the ordering

The reverse-topological ordering put this first on the strength of its
downstream edges. That was right, but for a different reason than I gave:
not because the template was structurally unstable, but because it was the
one node nothing regenerates, so every fix flowing through it stops there.

The edge that matters is not template -> instability. It is
template -> nothing regenerates it.
")))
